The cross-chat playback part of the voice-messages epic (#120, #137),
squashed from the incremental patch series. With
experimentalAudioPlayerControls off, the native re-parented <audio> bar
is byte-identical on every viewport.

Mini-player (wide layout): instead of re-parenting the native singleton
into the bar, the shared CustomAudioControls drive THE SINGLETON
directly (audioRef wraps ctx.audioElement in a stable useMemo so effects
don't re-subscribe per render; the bubble-side restore guard is a
live-playback no-op here and never rewinds the playing message). The
controls row gets flex:1 so the waveform fills the width left of the
close button. A clickable sender line (avatar + name + chat) jumps to
the message — same-account via jumpToMessage, cross-account via the
CommandPalette __selectAccount idiom. The sender row renders only for a
RESOLVED queued message (draft previews / in-flight fetches would show
an empty focusable jump button), reads the fresh fetch result (never the
lingering one — the bar must not attribute the playing message to the
previous sender), and the chat sub-line shows only when it differs from
the sender name (1:1 chats are named after the contact — "Bob / Bob").

Media Session (guarded, only while a src plays and the setting is on):
metadata is PRIVACY-SUPPRESSED by default — generic localized "Voice
message", no artist/artwork — because it can land on a lock screen
(forum 5423). voiceMessageMediaSessionDetails (default false) opts into
sender/chat/avatar; the setting lives in Settings -> Notifications right
below "show notification content" (same privacy question; key unchanged,
greyed out while the custom player is off). Handlers: play/pause,
seekback/forward +-10s clamped, seekto, nexttrack -> the existing
auto-advance (unregistered when there's no next). positionState on
timeupdate/ratechange/durationchange, ~1Hz throttle, try-caught
(setPositionState throws on non-finite duration), pushed immediately
after a seek. The idle branch of useMediaSession MUST stay
side-effect-free (KEEP when resolving conflicts): with the bar and the
strip both mounted, crossing the breakpoint runs the newly-idle
instance's setup AFTER the newly-active one's — clearing there wiped
the fresh registration and killed lock-screen controls; only the
previously-active run's cleanup clears (all cleanups run before any
setup).

Mobile strip (#137): on the single-column layout the chat-list column —
and with it the bar — is display:none while a chat is open, so playback
continued from an invisible surface. The player now renders as a slim
36px+2px strip pinned under the navbar, mounted in MainScreen OUTSIDE
both columns so it survives the chat-list/chat-view switch:
play/pause (13px inline padding — ~44px touch target), truncated
"sender · chat" label (tap = jump, spacer instead of a dead button when
there's nothing to jump to), speed pill on the shared ctx.playbackRate,
close, and a 2px aria-hidden progress hairline (a position readout, NOT
a control — the bubble owns seeking, the OS media session owns external
scrubbing, a 2px pointer target would be a bad third option). Content-
surface colors (bgPrimary/textPrimary), not the navbar's — the strip
reads as its own element below it (Telegram's pattern). The 38px band is
RESERVED, not overlaid: the strip is absolute (z-index 2 — above the
message list's local stacking scopes, below toast 100/dialogs) and a
sibling-combinator rule keyed off the strip's presence
([data-testid='voice-message-strip'] ~ …) gives both small-screen
navbars a 38px margin-bottom; overlaying would swallow taps on the
topmost chat row. No strip in the DOM -> the rule matches nothing.
Keep the strip as MainScreen's FIRST child — the ~ combinator depends
on it (tab-order trade-off documented inline).

One surface at a time: voicePlayerSurface(smallScreen, customControls,
hasSrc) in mediaSession.ts is the single decision — none / native /
strip / bar. Both mount points render GlobalVoiceMessagePlayer; the
non-current one returns null AND keeps its hooks idle, so sender fetch,
jump wiring and media-session registration never run twice. The strip is
its own component so its ~4Hz timeupdate re-renders don't reach the
player's fetch hooks; useAudioElementState is the extracted
paused/time/duration subscription shared with CustomAudioControls.

Pure helpers (mediaSessionMetadata, clampSeek, voicePlayerSurface) plus
the positionStore/utils cases are covered by the audioPlayer mocha
suite (lands here because it imports mediaSession.ts).
